<article class="env-lemma" id="0B8J">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0B8J">Lemma <span data-tag="0B8J">17.11.7</span>.</a> Let $(X, \mathcal{O}_ X)$ be a ringed space. Let $\mathcal{F}$ be a finitely presented $\mathcal{O}_ X$-module. Let $x \in X$ such that $\mathcal{F}_ x \cong \mathcal{O}_{X, x}^{\oplus r}$. Then there exists an open neighbourhood $U$ of $x$ such that $\mathcal{F}|_ U \cong \mathcal{O}_ U^{\oplus r}$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Choose $s_1, \ldots , s_ r \in \mathcal{F}_ x$ mapping to a basis of $\mathcal{O}_{X, x}^{\oplus r}$ by the isomorphism. Choose an open neighbourhood $U$ of $x$ such that $s_ i$ lifts to $s_ i \in \mathcal{F}(U)$. After shrinking $U$ we see that the induced map $\psi : \mathcal{O}_ U^{\oplus r} \to \mathcal{F}|_ U$ is surjective (Lemma <a href="/tag/01B8" data-tag="01B8">17.9.4</a>). By Lemma <a href="/tag/01BP" data-tag="01BP">17.11.4</a> we see that $\mathop{\mathrm{Ker}}(\psi )$ is of finite type. Then $\mathop{\mathrm{Ker}}(\psi )_ x = 0$ implies that $\mathop{\mathrm{Ker}}(\psi )$ becomes zero after shrinking $U$ once more (Lemma <a href="/tag/01B9" data-tag="01B9">17.9.5</a>). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>